Below is a brief description of what’s happening and three small *SEARCH/REPLACE* patches (for **bold**, **italic** and **strike**) in your `components/prism-markdown.js`. The core of the issue is that the old regex for `italic` (and its cousins) was “eating” any nested `*` or `_` characters, so an input like

    *__foo__*   or   _**bar**_

would be treated entirely as italic, preventing the inner `__…__` or `**…**` from ever becoming bold.  

**Root cause**  
Each of the three patterns was written as  

    /(^|[^\\])(<marker>)(?:…)+?\2/  

which matches an opening marker (`\2`), the entire contents (even other markers!) and the same marker again. Because of `greedy: true` it swallows everything, so nested markers never get a chance.  

**Fix**  
We turn the closing marker into its own capture `(\2)` and then use Prism’s `groups` mapping to tag **only** the two marker spans as punctuation. That way the middle (group 3) can be recursively tokenized (so nested bold inside italic or vice-versa now works).  

